<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Drelay.Launcher: starts the game suspended, injects the hook, resumes it.

    A console application rather than a windowed one: it is a diagnostic tool
    first, and its output is the evidence for Spike 1.1. It needs no elevation,
    because CreateProcess plus CreateRemoteThread on a process it created
    requires no privilege at all.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- net8.0 for the same reason as the bootstrap; see Drelay.Bootstrap.csproj. -->
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <AssemblyName>Drelay.Launcher</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>Drelay.Launcher</RootNamespace>
    <UseAppHost>true</UseAppHost>
    <AppendTargetFrameworkToOutputPath>false</AppendTargetFrameworkToOutputPath>
    <PublishSingleFile>false</PublishSingleFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The launcher references the bootstrap, and that is deliberate rather than
    convenient.

    Both processes have to agree on the shared block's layout and on its total
    size, and the size is a compile-time constant here: the launcher creates the
    mapping at that size, and the native DLL's sizeof(DrelayShared) must be the
    same number. A second copy of that constant in the launcher would be a second
    place for it to be wrong, and the failure it produces (a mapping one size and
    a reader another) is exactly the class of bug this design spends its effort
    turning into a loud refusal.

    So the layout lives once, in Drelay.Bootstrap\DrelayBlock.cs, and the launcher
    links against it. The bootstrap DLL is never loaded by the launcher; this is a
    compile-time dependency on a library of types, which is what it always was.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Drelay.Bootstrap\Drelay.Bootstrap.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
